$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The allegation</h2>
<p>The prosecution case arose from an incident in the informant's courtyard. The petitioner, allegedly acting on the instruction of co-accused Nathuni Sah, struck the informant on the head with a garasa — a sharp-edged implement — leaving a scalp wound of about three inches, bone-deep, medically described as grievous. Other accused were said to have assaulted the informant, attempted to strangle him, and snatched a gold locket. The FIR invoked Sections 341, 323, 324, 307 and 504 read with 34 of the Indian Penal Code, the case having been registered in 2020, before the new criminal codes came into force.</p>

<h2>The competing considerations</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Against bail</h3>
    <p>A grievous, bone-deep head injury attributed with a specific role; an attempt-to-murder charge under Section 307 IPC; the State's grievance that the petitioner never surrendered after his anticipatory bail pleas were rejected and thereby delayed the trial by about six years; one prior case on record.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>For bail</h3>
    <p>Custody since 9 June 2026; chargesheet already filed; the plea of false implication to be tested at trial; co-accused granted anticipatory bail as far back as 2021; trial yet to conclude, with the petitioner now available to face it.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The Court granted bail on bonds of Rs. 10,000 with two sureties of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the Chief Judicial Magistrate, Darbhanga, or the court concerned. The order is a reminder that pre-trial detention is not a punishment for earlier evasion: once the investigation is complete and a meaningful spell of custody has been undergone, the question becomes whether continued detention serves any purpose the conditions of bail cannot.</p>

<h2>The conditions imposed</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Statutory conditions:</strong> the petitioner is bound by Section 480(3) of the BNSS — not to commit a similar offence, not to tamper with evidence or influence witnesses.</li>
  <li><strong>Close-relative bailor:</strong> one of the two bailors must be a close relative, tying the family to the petitioner's compliance.</li>
  <li><strong>Attendance:</strong> appearance on every date fixed by the trial court is mandatory.</li>
  <li><strong>Consequence of default:</strong> absence on three consecutive dates, or breach of any bail term, may lead to cancellation of the bail bond.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Reading the order as precedent for practice</h2>
<p>For the defence, the order illustrates the sequencing that often works in injury cases carrying a Section 307 charge: once anticipatory bail fails, prolonged evasion compounds the problem, but a surrender followed by a few months of custody and the filing of the chargesheet creates a fresh and stronger footing for regular bail. For the prosecution and the informant, it shows where the leverage lies after bail is granted — strict enforcement of attendance conditions, and a cancellation application the moment the accused treats liberty casually. The six years the State says were lost to abscondence were not ignored; they were priced into the conditions rather than into further detention.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> A bail order of this kind decides nothing about guilt. The grievous-injury opinion, the attributed role and the plea of false implication all remain open for trial. What the order fixes is the framework of liberty: bonds, a family surety, and a short leash on attendance.</p>
</div>
